    "bodyMarkdown": "## Two moisturisers built around ceramides\n\n[Skin Renewal SR Peptide Recovery Cream](/reviews/sr-peptide-recovery-cream) and [COR Skincare Ceramide Barrier Moisturiser](/reviews/cor-ceramide-barrier-moisturiser) are the kind of pair a South African shopper really does weigh up: both lean on ceramides, they suit overlapping skin types, and one costs about 3.0× the other (R225 for COR Skincare, R680 for Skin Renewal). That price gap is the question this showdown answers: what you get, and don't get, for the extra money.\n\n## What's in each\n\n| | Skin Renewal SR Peptide Recovery Cream | COR Skincare Ceramide Barrier Moisturiser |\n|---|---|---|\n| Key ingredients | Peptides, Ceramides | Ceramides, Cholesterol, Fatty Acids |\n| Indicative price | R680 | R225 |\n| Suits | Normal, Mature, Compromised | Dry, Sensitive, Normal |\n| SkinLabs overall score | 7.3/10 | 7.6/10 |\n\nBoth formulas list ceramides-related ingredients among their key actives (Peptides, Ceramides for Skin Renewal; Ceramides, Cholesterol, Fatty Acids for COR Skincare), so the real differences come from the rest of each formula and from how it behaves on skin.\n\n## How the SkinLabs scores compare\n\nWe score every product on four things, independently: efficacy, value, texture and fit for South African conditions. Here's how this pair lines up:\n\n| Score (out of 10) | Skin Renewal | COR Skincare |\n|---|---|---|\n| Efficacy | 7.5 | 7.0 |\n| Value | 6.0 | 7.5 |\n| Texture | 8.0 | 8.0 |\n| SA climate fit | 7.5 | 8.0 |\n\nSkin Renewal scores higher on efficacy (7.5 vs 7.0). COR Skincare scores higher on value (7.5 vs 6.0). On texture they're effectively level (8.0 vs 8.0). COR Skincare scores higher on SA climate fit (8.0 vs 7.5). These scores reflect SkinLabs' editorial assessment of publicly available ingredient and pricing information, not laboratory or clinical testing.\n\n## Who each one suits\n\nBoth list normal skin as a match. Skin Renewal additionally lists mature and compromised skin. COR Skincare additionally lists dry and sensitive skin. Our verdicts, in each review's own words: Skin Renewal — A solid, if unremarkable, peptide barrier-support cream — dependable clinic-grade formulation at clinic-grade pricing. COR Skincare — A solid, unfussy barrier-support cream that layers cleanly under the niacinamide serum — does one job well rather than chasing a long ingredient list, which suits COR's whole modest-claims approach.\n\n## Price and where to buy\n\nCOR Skincare is the cheaper option at R225; Skin Renewal sits at R680. Prices are indicative and change often. Skin Renewal is listed at Brand Direct, and COR Skincare at Brand Direct. COR Skincare also scores at least as well on value (7.5 vs 6.0), so the cheaper product is not a compromise on that front.\n\n## Our take\n\nThere's no universal winner here. Pick Skin Renewal if efficacy matters most to you; pick COR Skincare if value and SA climate fit does. Whichever you choose, patch-test a new product first, and remember this is cosmetic guidance, not medical advice.",
