    "bodyMarkdown": "## Two moisturisers built around shea butter\n\n[Lamelle Serra Restore Cream](/reviews/lamelle-serra-restore-cream) and [Black African Organics Shea Deep Moisture Cream](/reviews/bao-shea-deep-moisture-cream) are the kind of pair a South African shopper really does weigh up: both lean on shea butter, they suit overlapping skin types, and one costs about 3.2× the other (R210 for Black African Organics, R665 for Lamelle). That price gap is the question this showdown answers: what you get, and don't get, for the extra money.\n\n## What's in each\n\n| | Lamelle Serra Restore Cream | Black African Organics Shea Deep Moisture Cream |\n|---|---|---|\n| Key ingredients | Ceramide-P, Panthenol, Oat Bran Extract, Shea Butter | Shea Butter, Glycerin |\n| Indicative price | R665 | R210 |\n| Suits | Dry, Sensitive, Eczema, Compromised | Dry, All |\n| SkinLabs overall score | 8.1/10 | 7.5/10 |\n\nBoth formulas list shea butter-related ingredients among their key actives (Ceramide-P, Panthenol, Oat Bran Extract, Shea Butter for Lamelle; Shea Butter, Glycerin for Black African Organics), so the real differences come from the rest of each formula and from how it behaves on skin.\n\n## How the SkinLabs scores compare\n\nWe score every product on four things, independently: efficacy, value, texture and fit for South African conditions. Here's how this pair lines up:\n\n| Score (out of 10) | Lamelle | Black African Organics |\n|---|---|---|\n| Efficacy | 8.5 | 7.0 |\n| Value | 7.0 | 8.0 |\n| Texture | 8.5 | 8.0 |\n| SA climate fit | 8.5 | 7.0 |\n\nLamelle scores higher on efficacy (8.5 vs 7.0). Black African Organics scores higher on value (8.0 vs 7.0). Lamelle scores higher on texture (8.5 vs 8.0). Lamelle scores higher on SA climate fit (8.5 vs 7.0). These scores reflect SkinLabs' editorial assessment of publicly available ingredient and pricing information, not laboratory or clinical testing.\n\n## Who each one suits\n\nBoth list dry skin as a match. Lamelle additionally lists sensitive, eczema and compromised skin. Black African Organics additionally lists all skin. Our verdicts, in each review's own words: Lamelle — An excellent, evidence-driven lamellar barrier cream built around patented Ceramide-P — premium priced, but the formulation quality justifies it for compromised skin. Black African Organics — A rich, effective daily moisturiser for drier melanin-rich skin — traditional botanicals blended with modern humectants.\n\n## Price and where to buy\n\nBlack African Organics is the cheaper option at R210; Lamelle sits at R665. Prices are indicative and change often. Lamelle is listed at Dermastore, SkinMiles, and Black African Organics at Brand Direct. Black African Organics also scores at least as well on value (8.0 vs 7.0), so the cheaper product is not a compromise on that front.\n\n## Our take\n\nThere's no universal winner here. Pick Lamelle if efficacy and texture and SA climate fit matters most to you; pick Black African Organics if value does. Whichever you choose, patch-test a new product first, and remember this is cosmetic guidance, not medical advice.",
